let's start today with mass shootings  you know whenever there is news of a  specific mass shooting understandably  the focus becomes the facts of that  particular situation when we had the  buffalo mass shooting where this 18 year  old was targeting black people this was  in buffalo new york not this past  weekend but the previous one we  immediately understandably correctly  spoke about the details of that incident  where did the individual come from what  were the motivations of the individual  who were the victims of what was the  response what has been the political  response and on and on and whenever we  do that  we often will lose  the perspective  that there are so many of these mass  shootings in the united states that it  would almost be a full daily podcast  just to keep up with the mass shootings  that are taking place in the united  states now i spoke to a couple of people  yesterday just casually texted friends  and said hey i'm researching for a story  i'm doing  if i just asked how many mass shootings  have there been in 2022 so far without  looking anything up what's your sense  and many of them wrote back something  like well i'm aware of like three or  four specific ones that like made the  news to the to the threshold that i was  aware of it so there's probably been  like 40 or 50 because we have a huge  mass shooting problem here in the united  states we have had as of yesterday  210 mass shootings in the united states  so far it's only may  it's only may and we have had 210 mass  shootings never mind individual  shootings as well as shootings in which  two but not more than two people were  injured or killed remember if one person  shoots two people  by most metrics that's not considered a  mass shooting you could argue that it  should be  um but depending on the archive or or  statistics you look at it only is  considered a mass shooting when three or  four people  are  injured or killed and bearing that in  mind if you go to the gun violence  archive and you can download a  spreadsheet of this  you will see  210 actually in fact  i believe the number is now 211. since i  last looked at this there's there was a  mass shooting yesterday in south  carolina north charleston where five  people were injured so it is now 211  mass shootings in the united states  every single one of these is a story  that impacts  many people families friends co-workers  etc if we pick a random one of these  just for example the the  second most recent one  um which when i looked yesterday was the  most recent one when you look at that  one that was in goshen indiana by the  way a place i've been to  and um  it took place  on rosemary  court  um  when you look at the victims there was a  17 year old male who was killed  there was a female injured another  female injured another female injured  and the suspect is believed to be a 20  year old  male  who was also killed according to the  information that we have here and for  every single one of these typically  there is a media source that you can  look at in this case the media source  takes us  uh looks like it's a facebook page a  release from the goshen police  department talking about how an  emergency call was received  that a shooting took place at a  residence  five individuals were injured according  to the report emts were sent out two  injured females were airlifted to a  trauma hospital in fort wayne one was  taken to a hospital in south bend an  injured male was taken to goshen  hospital but has been declared deceased  and one male was pronounced deceased at  the scene  every single one of these is a story  that affects so many people's lives and  most of these don't even make national  news  and one of the things i've talked about  before uh npr also about nine days ago  did a story on this at that point nine  days ago there had been 198 mass  shootings now we're already up to 211.  whenever there's a news story  that doesn't have a clear beginning  middle and end  it often will not make it into the news  or whenever there's an event that like  that so when we talk about well  there was a shooting where one person  was killed and it gets more attention  than thousands of people in the united  states or however many are going hungry  whereas that's a major story how do we  still have hunger in a country as rich  as the united states well it comes to  what makes a good story for corporate  media and i've spoken about this before  uh terrorist attacks weather events  individual shootings votes on a  particular bill  those have a defined typically beginning  middle and end  they have very clear players  there are very clear sort of goal posts  for what is the coverage expected to be  and how are these topics discussed  uh regular corporate media is not really  set up to talk about  hunger in the united states  in an ongoing way okay there's a food  stamp bill democrats for republicans i  guess whatever but the news corporate  for-profit news is not really set up to  talk about that uh uh malaria in africa  um  dysentery and  these are not the types of stories  that corporate media is designed to  address and even when you look at the  politics the political stories  the  intractable and corrosive nature of  how we select our representatives and  then how they don't really represent our  interests that's an ongoing and major  story arguably it's one of the  fundamental stories about how american  politics is broken it doesn't really  have a clear beginning middle and end  and as a result it's why you often don't  see much about it in corporate news so  to go back to the beginning 210 211 now  mass shootings in the united states and  it is not even memorial day  sponsoring today's show is the new book  by jason murchie called wisdom a very  valuable virtue that cannot be bought  this is an excellent book i've been  reading through  no book can really teach you how to be  wise but this is a really well  researched nonfiction book that helps  readers  grow to love the pursuit of wisdom which  is really a process at the end of the  day and if you spend just two minutes  watching my show  you'll know america has a significant  lack of wisdom in many ways republicans  have made an art form out of failing to  think critically they have their  flexible and exclusive values moral  dogmatism  right now is probably one of the low  points in this particular sense in  american history with trump tribalism  the pandemic and just the the lack of  wise thinking  the author of the book jason murchie  believes that while liberals ought to  aspire to be tolerant and compassionate  right-wing extremism and political  chicanery must be resisted and overcome  and mercy really brings a refreshing  array of insightful and interesting  quotations and personal reflections it's  a  deep and wide investigation into what  wisdom is  i've been really enjoying reading  through it it's  really easy to read in chunks  each chapter kind of stands on its own  so you can jump into the book very  quickly it's a book that could have a  really powerful impact on a lot of  readers pick up a copy at  davidpakman.com  wisdom  the link is down below

